<body>
<h1>AI Internal Linking</h1>
<p class="sub">What it does, how it does it, and why it picks one page and one phrase rather than another.
Written to be read without any technical background. Version 0.22.0.</p>

<h2>1. What this plugin does</h2>

<p>It reads every page on your site once, then works through them one at a time asking a single question: is
there a sentence here that should link to another page of mine, and which words should carry that link?
Anything it finds is a <em>suggestion</em>. Nothing is inserted until you approve it, nothing is ever written
for you, and every link it does insert can be undone.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>The rule everything else obeys.</strong> The plugin can only link words already
written on your page. It never adds a sentence, never rephrases you, and never invents a phrase to link. If a
good destination exists but your text contains no natural phrase to carry the link, no suggestion is made at
all. That one rule explains most of what follows.</div>

<h2>2. The words this document uses</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Word</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Source page</strong></td><td>The page being read. The link will be added <em>to</em> this page.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Destination</strong></td><td>The page being linked <em>to</em>. Also called the target.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shortlist</strong></td><td>The small group of pages worth considering as a destination for one
particular source page. Your site might have 350 pages; only a dozen or two could sensibly be linked from any
given article.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wording</strong></td><td>The words that become clickable. In "your content marketing strategy
should be planned", the wording might be "content marketing strategy". Often called anchor text.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>A number from 0 to 1: do these two pages belong together? Section 9
explains how each engine arrives at it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Naturalness</strong></td><td>A number from 0 to 1: does this particular wording read well as a
link? Judged from the phrase alone.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>One more, only if you use the AI engine. A <strong>token</strong> is roughly three quarters of a word. AI
providers bill by the token, so a 1,000 word page costs about 1,300 tokens to send. That is the only reason
the word appears in this document.</p>

<h2>3. The whole process, in order</h2>

<p>Six stages. Each narrows what is possible, and each can end with nothing &mdash; which is why a page you
expected to be linked sometimes is not.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Section</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Read the site</td><td>Every page is read once and its words recorded</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Pick a source page</td><td>Pages out of scope, or already full of links, are skipped</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Build a shortlist</td><td>The pages that could plausibly be the destination</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Settle the destination</td><td>Three engines, in a fixed order, each with different evidence</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Settle the wording</td><td>A phrase that already exists in the source page</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Score it</td><td>Relevance and naturalness, blended into the figure you see</td><td>9</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The image <code>ai-selection-flowchart.png</code> beside this document shows the same sequence as a diagram,
including every branch where a suggestion is thrown away.</p>
<h2>4. Stage 1: how your site is read</h2>

<p><strong>Internally, from your WordPress database. It never requests your own pages over the internet.</strong></p>

<p>The plugin asks WordPress for each post directly, reads page-builder content from the builder's own stored
fields, and reduces the result to plain words. The only outside connections it ever makes are to Google Search
Console and to the AI provider you configured, if any.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Because it reads the database</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr><td>No page requests</td><td>Indexing is fast and puts no load on your site</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caching cannot interfere</td><td>It always sees your current content, never a stale cached copy</td></tr>
<tr><td>No public address needed</td><td>Works on staging and password-protected sites</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The trade-off, stated plainly</h3>

<p>Content that only exists when a page is displayed is invisible to it: theme-added sections, related-post
blocks, and the output of other plugins' shortcodes. Shortcode tags are removed and any text between them is
kept, but what a shortcode would have generated is not.</p>

<p>That is deliberate. The plugin should only ever link words that live in your content and that you can edit.
A link placed in theme output would vanish the day you change theme.</p>

<h3>What gets recorded</h3>

<p>For each page: its title, its address, its language, its plain text, and a count of how often each
meaningful word appears. Headings and the page title are stripped out before that count is taken, so they can
never become a link later.</p>

<h2>5. Stage 2: choosing which page to work on</h2>

<p>The plugin works through your pages one at a time. A page is skipped before anything else happens if either
of these is true.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Skipped when</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Its content type is not ticked under "Crawl these post types"</td><td>Your choice, on the Setup &amp;
Dashboard screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>It already has all the links its length allows</td><td>5 links per 1,000 words by default, and never
fewer than 2 for any page. Links already in your content count towards it</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The second matters more than it looks. It protects pages that are already well linked, and it is why a
long-established article often produces nothing while a new one produces several suggestions.</p>
<h2>6. Stage 3: building the shortlist</h2>

<p>This is where "why that page and not another" is actually decided, and it happens <em>before</em> any engine
chooses a destination. Even with the AI switched on, the AI does not search your site: it is handed this
shortlist and may only choose from it.</p>

<h3>Step 1: which pages are eligible at all</h3>

<p>Eligibility is absolute. A page is never considered as a destination if any of these is true:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Ruled out when</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>It is the page being read</td><td>Nothing links to itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is not published</td><td>Drafts, private and pending pages are not destinations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Its content type is not ticked as a link target</td><td>Your choice, on the Setup &amp; Dashboard screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>You marked it excluded</td><td>Your choice</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is a WooCommerce cart, checkout or account page</td><td>Never a useful destination</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is in a different language</td><td>An English page is never offered a Hindi destination, on any
multilingual setup</td></tr>
<tr><td>It shares fewer than two meaningful words with the source page</td><td>One word in common is
coincidence, not a relationship</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>Worth reading twice.</strong> A page can be an obvious match by any human judgement
and still never appear, because of one of the rules above. In practice the usual culprit is the third: the
page's content type was never ticked as a link target. Nothing about the wording or the subject can rescue
it.</div>

<h3>Step 2: what is compared</h3>

<p>Everything still standing is compared on <strong>vocabulary, and only on vocabulary</strong>. Not
categories, not tags, not dates, not how the pages are linked today. Just the words &mdash; and not even all of
those. Set aside first:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Ignored</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>Filler words such as "the", "and", "with"</td><td>Present everywhere, so they distinguish nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anything shorter than three letters</td><td>Same reason</td></tr>
<tr><td>Any word appearing on more than 40 percent of your pages</td><td>If most of your site uses a word, it
cannot tell two pages apart. On a cooking site, "recipe" is ignored automatically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headings and the page title</td><td>Removed when the page is first read, so they never enter the
comparison</td></tr>
</table>

<p>What remains is a page's <strong>distinctive vocabulary</strong>. The source page is represented by its 40
most-used surviving words; every page it is compared against, by up to 300 of its own.</p>

<h3>Step 3: how "closest" is decided</h3>

<pre class="formula">For each word the two pages share:

    how rare that word is across your whole site
      x  the smaller of the two pages' usage counts

Add all of those up, then divide by the source page's own total.

The result is between 0 and 1: the share of this page's distinctive
vocabulary that the other page also covers.</pre>

<p>Two details there do the real work. <strong>Rarity</strong> means a shared unusual word counts for far more
than a shared ordinary one, which is what stops every page on your site looking related to every other.
<strong>The smaller of the two counts</strong> stops a page that repeats one word two hundred times from
appearing related to everything that mentions it once.</p>

<p>Every eligible page is scored this way, sorted highest first, and the best become the shortlist.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>In one sentence:</strong> the shortlist is the pages that share the most distinctive
vocabulary with the page being read, out of everything published, in the same language, of a content type you
chose as a link target.</div>

<h3>Step 4: how long the shortlist is</h3>

<p>Your choice. "Potential Destination Pages shown to AI" on the Setup &amp; Dashboard screen starts at 15 and
accepts anything from 5 to 200.</p>

<p><strong>Why not fewer.</strong> A destination that is not on the list cannot be chosen, however obviously
right it is. <strong>Why not more.</strong> A page ranked fiftieth by shared wording is down there precisely
because it has little in common, so a longer list is mostly weaker options &mdash; and costs more to send.</p>

<p>The list is filtered after it is built: the page itself is removed, so is anything it already links to, and
so is any pair you have already approved or rejected. The plugin therefore always fetches noticeably more than
it will show, in proportion to the number you chose.</p>
<h2>7. Stage 4: settling the destination</h2>

<p>Three engines run in a fixed order for each source page. Each proposes links until the page's link budget is
used up, so a later engine only sees whatever capacity the earlier ones left.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Order</th><th>Engine</th><th>The question it answers</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Does this page mention a phrase another of my pages already ranks for,
without linking to it?</td><td>Google Search Console</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>Reading this page, which of these related pages would an editor link
to?</td><td>A chat model, using your own key</td><td>Your API key</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Related Content</td><td>Which pages talk about the same things as this one?</td><td>Word
comparison, on your own server</td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<p>They run in that order because the evidence weakens as you go down. What people actually search for beats
what a model thinks, and what a model thinks beats counting shared words. The last one runs on every page
regardless, so you always get suggestions even with no key and no Search Console.</p>

<h3>7a. GSC keyword &mdash; runs first</h3>

<p class="sub">Free. Needs Google Search Console connected.</p>

<p><strong>Neither half of this link is chosen by the plugin.</strong> Google chooses the destination, because
Search Console already knows which of your pages answers a given search. The people searching choose the
wording, because it is the phrase they typed.</p>

<h4>Step 1: how much is a search phrase worth?</h4>

<p>Not every phrase is worth spending a link on. A phrase you already rank first for has nothing to gain. A
phrase you rank 80th for is out of reach. The useful ones sit in between.</p>

<pre class="formula">Worth = impressions x (click rate at position 3 - click rate at your position)
        then reduced the further you are from the top ten</pre>

<table>
<tr><th>You currently rank</th><th>1st</th><th>3rd</th><th>5th</th><th>10th</th><th>20th</th><th>40th</th><th>99th</th></tr>
<tr><td>Worth, per 1,000 people who saw it</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>50</td><td>85</td><td>102</td><td>53</td><td>21</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Ranking 1st scores zero because you have already won. Ranking 20th scores highest, because you are one nudge
from the top of page one. Ranking 99th scores low, because no single internal link will fix that.</p>

<h4>Step 2: turning that into relevance</h4>

<pre class="formula">relevance = 0.50   always, for any Search Console match
          + 0.20   if you rank between 5th and 20th
          + up to 0.28   in proportion to the worth above
          capped at 0.98</pre>

<p>The starting point of 0.50 is deliberate. A phrase people genuinely search for is stronger evidence than any
amount of word similarity, so these suggestions begin above what the other engines normally reach.</p>

<h4>How the link is chosen</h4>

<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th></th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Match the address Google reports to one of your pages</td><td>Dropped. A page the plugin
cannot identify is unusable</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>The destination is now settled, chosen by Google</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>The wording is now settled: the search phrase itself</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Is the phrase between 2 and 4 words?</td><td>Skipped. One word is not descriptive, nine is a
sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Does the source page actually contain that phrase?</td><td>Skipped. Nothing to turn into a
link</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Does it already link there?</td><td>Skipped. Then it is not a missed link</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Would this be the fourth identical wording pointing at the same page?</td><td>Skipped.
Repeating the same wording looks manipulative to a search engine</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Only the 500 most valuable phrases are considered per scan, and each destination gets at most one keyword
suggestion per page, so the best phrase wins.</p>
<h3>7b. AI Suggestion &mdash; runs second</h3>

<p class="sub">Optional. Needs an API key, which is yours and stays yours.</p>

<p>By the time the model is asked anything, your site has already been read in full and the shortlist from
Section 6 already exists. The model receives your page's own text together with that shortlist.</p>

<pre class="formula">Sent to the model   your page's title
                    the opening N words of your page
                      (your setting: 1,000 by default, up to 20,000)
                    a numbered list of possible destinations
                      (your setting: 15 by default, from 5 to 200)

Each entry is       the page's title, followed by a short summary of
                    what that page covers
                      (your setting: 40 words by default, from 25 to 200;
                       or a list of key words, or the title alone)

Reply limit         short, roughly 600 words
Creativity          turned off, so the same page gives the same answer</pre>

<h4>Where those summaries come from</h4>

<p>They are <strong>taken from the page, not written for it</strong>. Nothing is generated and no second AI
request is made. The plugin scores every sentence on the destination page against that page's own distinctive
vocabulary &mdash; the same vocabulary worked out in Section 6 &mdash; and keeps the two or three that
represent it best.</p>

<p>One consequence explains why the summaries are usable at all: <strong>wording your whole site repeats can
never appear in one</strong>. Words used across the site are set aside before scoring, so a sentence built from
them scores zero however often it appears. Navigation text, standard introductions and "in this article you
will learn" blocks are ruled out automatically, not by a list of things to avoid.</p>

<p>Sentences that ask rather than tell are pushed down too, and so are the lines immediately after them. In a
multiple-choice question some of the options are deliberately false, and they read as ordinary statements
&mdash; only their position after the question gives them away.</p>

<h4>What the model is shown, and what it must return</h4>

<pre class="formula">SHOWN TO THE MODEL                        THE MODEL REPLIES

Article title: Build a Content Calendar     Page number: 1
Article text:  ...your content marketing    Wording:     content marketing strategy
strategy stays consistent...                Reason:      the article plans a strategy
                                            Confidence:  0.86
Possible destinations:
  1. Content Marketing Strategy: A Guide - A content strategy sets out who
     you are writing for and why. Pillar pages anchor each theme.
  2. Social Media Scheduling Tools - Scheduling tools queue posts across
     networks from one place.</pre>

<p><strong>Notice what is missing: there are no web addresses anywhere.</strong> The model is shown titles and
summaries and replies with a number. The plugin then looks up which page number 1 is and takes the address from
its own records. The model never handles a web address, so it cannot invent one.</p>

<h4>What the plugin does with that reply</h4>

<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th></th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Is the page number one it was actually shown?</td><td>Thrown away. A number outside the list
means nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Look up that page; its address comes from the plugin's records</td><td>Cannot fail. The model
had no say in the address</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Is it a different page from the one being read, and not already used?</td><td>Thrown away</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Search the article for the wording it quoted, whole words, ignoring capitals</td><td>Thrown
away. This is the step that kills invented wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Keep the wording exactly as your article writes it, plus the sentence around it</td><td>Cannot
fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Use the model's confidence as the relevance score</td><td>0.70 assumed if it gives none</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Why it is built this way.</strong> A model asked to produce a web address will eventually produce a
believable one that does not exist. A model asked to produce wording will eventually tidy up your phrasing.
Neither can happen here: the address never leaves the plugin, and step 4 above rejects any wording the model
did not copy exactly.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>The one limitation worth knowing.</strong> The model reads the beginning of a page,
not all of it. At the default of 1,000 words, a 3,000 word article is about one third visible, so this engine
suggests links from the opening section. Increase "Words per page sent to the AI" to widen that. The other two
engines always read the whole page.</div>

<h3>7c. Related Content &mdash; runs last</h3>

<p class="sub">Free, always available, no key, no internet connection.</p>

<p>This engine runs on every page no matter what, and fills whatever link budget the other two leave. It also
builds the shortlist the AI reads, so it is working even when the AI is switched on.</p>

<p>Its relevance score is exactly the comparison already described in Section 6, step 3.</p>

<p>It works in the <strong>opposite order</strong> to the other two. It settles the destination first, then
tries to work out the wording, and gives up on the pair if it cannot.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th></th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Score every eligible page and sort them, best first</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Take the best one left, skipping the page itself and anything already linked, suggested or
rejected</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Ignore anything below the minimum relevance. Below that, shared words are coincidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Take that page's <em>title</em>, cut it at any punctuation, and build every word group from 4
words down to 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Throw away groups starting or ending on a filler word, containing a bare number, or
containing words like "part" or "chapter"</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Try the longest remaining group first. The first one that appears in your page becomes the
wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>If none appears, give up on this pair entirely and try the next page, however high the
score</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Why longest first.</strong> "content marketing strategy" tells a reader where the link goes.
"strategy" does not. Trying longer groups first means the most descriptive available wording always wins.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>This is the most common reason a link you expected never appears.</strong> Step 7. Two
pages can be obviously about the same subject and still produce nothing, simply because your text never happens
to contain a phrase from the other page's title.</div>

<p><strong>Nothing here knows your subject.</strong> The vocabulary is whatever your own pages contain, and the
rarity scores are worked out from your own site each time you scan. The identical method produces recipe links
on a cooking site and case-law links on a legal one.</p>
<h2>8. Stage 5: settling the wording</h2>

<p>Each engine arrives at the wording differently, but one rule closes every path.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>Chooses the destination</th><th>Chooses the wording</th><th>In what order</th></tr>
<tr><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Google</td><td>The people searching</td><td>The destination arrives attached to the
phrase</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>The model, by picking a number from a shortlist someone else built</td><td>The
model quotes a phrase; your page supplies the exact text</td><td>Both at once</td></tr>
<tr><td>Related Content</td><td>Word comparison</td><td>The destination page's own title</td><td>Destination
first, wording worked out afterwards</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="key"><strong>The rule that closes every path:</strong> the wording must already exist on your page,
matched as whole words, ignoring capitals. If it is not there, no suggestion is made. That check happens after
the engine has had its say, which is why it catches anything a model invents.</div>

<p>It also means <strong>the saved wording is always yours</strong>. Whatever the model typed, or however Google
reported the phrase, what gets saved is the wording exactly as your article writes it. The model wrote "content
marketing strategy" in lower case; your page says "Content Marketing Strategy", so that is what is stored and
that is what gets linked.</p>

<h3>Where a link may never be placed</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Never linked</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Inside a heading</td><td>Headings are stripped before anything is read, so they cannot even be
proposed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inside an existing link</td><td>Links cannot nest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inside code or preformatted text</td><td>The text there is not prose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inside a tag's attributes</td><td>It would break the tag rather than link anything</td></tr>
<tr><td>Across a partial word</td><td>"strategy" never matches inside "strategyx"</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>9. Stage 6: the two scores</h2>

<p>Every suggestion carries two numbers answering different questions. They are worked out independently, so
the blend at the end means what it says.</p>

<h3>Relevance: should these two pages be connected at all?</h3>

<p>It says nothing about how the link reads &mdash; only whether the two pages belong together. Each engine has
its own evidence, so each works it out differently:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>How relevance is decided</th><th>Typical range</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>GSC keyword</strong></td><td>Starts at 0.50 for every match, then rises with the traffic the
phrase could realistically gain</td><td>0.50 to 0.98</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>AI Suggestion</strong></td><td>The model's own stated confidence in the pick. 0.70 assumed if
it gives none</td><td>0.35 to 0.96</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Related Content</strong></td><td>The share of the source page's distinctive vocabulary that the
destination also uses</td><td>0.08 to 0.50</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>The three numbers are not comparable with each other.</strong> A 0.85 from the AI is
the model's opinion. A 0.85 from Related Content would mean two pages share most of their distinctive wording,
which almost never happens. Judge each engine against others of its own kind &mdash; which is why the review
screen labels the source of every suggestion.</div>

<p><strong>The floor, and what it governs.</strong> "Minimum relevance" on the Settings screen discards Related
Content pairs below it, and it applies to that engine alone. AI and Search Console suggestions are judged on
different evidence and never pass through it. The default is deliberately low, because a stricter test runs
afterwards regardless: the wording has to be found in your page, and that rejects far more pairs than the score
ever does.</p>

<h3>Naturalness: does this wording read well as a link?</h3>

<p>This looks only at the phrase that would become clickable. It knows nothing about relevance.</p>

<pre class="formula">Naturalness   start at                                    0.55
              add 0.20  if the wording is 2 to 4 words
              take off 0.15  if it is 5 words or more
              add 0.15  if it is 8 to 60 characters long
              take off 0.30  if it is under 5 characters
              take off 0.25  if it is over 70 characters

Overall       0.60 x relevance   +   0.40 x naturalness</pre>

<p>A single word is neither rewarded nor punished. Chinese, Japanese and Thai do not put spaces between words,
so every phrase in those languages would otherwise count as one word and every suggestion on such a site would
be marked down for the writing system it uses. Length is counted in characters rather than bytes for the same
reason: a three-character Hindi or Arabic phrase is nine bytes.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>What the percentage in the review screen is.</strong> The two blended, 60 percent
relevance and 40 percent naturalness. Well-formed wording tends to score alike, so most of the movement between
suggestions comes from relevance. That is intended: acceptable wording is a condition to be met, while whether
the pages belong together is the actual judgement.</div>

<h2>10. The limits every suggestion must pass</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Limit</th><th>Default</th><th>Where to change it</th><th>Why it exists</th></tr>
<tr><td>Links per 1,000 words</td><td>5, never fewer than 2 per page</td><td>Settings</td><td>Stops a page
turning into a wall of links</td></tr>
<tr><td>New suggestions per page</td><td>8 from any one scan</td><td>Settings</td><td>Keeps a review session
manageable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wording length</td><td>2 to 4 words</td><td>Settings</td><td>Descriptive, but never a whole
sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Same wording to the same page</td><td>3</td><td>fixed</td><td>Repetition looks manipulative to a search
engine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lowest relevance accepted</td><td>0.08, Related Content only</td><td>Settings</td><td>Below this,
shared words are coincidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Words of the page read by the AI</td><td>1,000, up to 20,000</td><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>The
main cost lever</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pages on the shortlist</td><td>15, from 5 to 200</td><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>How much the AI
may choose between</td></tr>
<tr><td>How each destination is described</td><td>a 40-word summary</td><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>How
well the AI can tell destinations apart</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11. A worked example, end to end</h2>

<p>Your page mentions "content marketing strategy". Another page is titled "Content Marketing Strategy: A
Complete Guide".</p>

<pre class="formula">1. The other page is eligible: published, a chosen content type,
   same language, and shares more than two meaningful words.

2. Vocabulary comparison scores it       relevance   = 0.42
   It is high enough to make the shortlist.

3. The title splits at the colon, giving "Content Marketing Strategy".

4. That 3 word phrase is searched for in your page. It is there,
   outside any heading, link or code block, so it can be the wording.

5. naturalness = 0.55  starting point
               + 0.20  3 words, so 2 to 4              = 0.75
               + 0.15  26 characters, within range     = 0.90

6. Overall     = (0.60 x 0.42) + (0.40 x 0.90)         = 0.61</pre>

<p>It reaches your review screen showing 0.42 relevance, 0.90 naturalness and 0.61 overall, with the
surrounding sentence quoted so you can judge the placement before approving anything.</p>
<h2>12. Applying a link, and undoing it</h2>

<p>Nothing is inserted without your approval. When you do approve, the plugin writes a WordPress revision
<em>and</em> an independent backup record before touching the post, so every insertion has two ways back. One
click undoes a single link, and a batched action removes every link the plugin ever inserted.</p>

<p>The insertion itself is a precise splice of a plain link tag at an exact position. Every other byte of your
content is left untouched, and each write passes a visible-text check before it is saved: if anything other
than the new link would change, the write is refused.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Editor</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gutenberg and Classic</td><td>Applied automatically when you approve</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elementor, Divi, WPBakery, Beaver Builder, ACF</td><td>Suggested only. The plugin never rewrites
content a builder owns; add the link manually using the wording and sentence shown</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Uninstalling restores content from the backup record first, so removing the plugin does not leave its links
behind.</p>

<h2>13. What it will never do</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Never</th><th>Why that matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Write or rewrite a sentence</td><td>Only words already in your page can become a link</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insert a link without approval</td><td>Every suggestion waits in the review screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invent a web address</td><td>The model never sees one; addresses come from the plugin's own
records</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link a heading or the page title</td><td>Both are removed before anything is read</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link inside existing links, code, or tag attributes</td><td>Structurally refused</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link across languages</td><td>An English page is never linked to a Hindi one</td></tr>
<tr><td>Send your content anywhere without a key</td><td>With no API key set up, the plugin makes no outside
connections at all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leave changes behind on uninstall</td><td>Content is restored from the backup record first</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>14. What you see in the admin</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Tab</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>Which content to crawl and link to, what the AI is given, indexing and
scanning, and a live cost estimate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suggestions</td><td>The review screen: approve, reject, apply, undo &mdash; one at a time or in bulk,
filtered by status and by which engine produced them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link Health</td><td>Orphans, dead ends, broken links, over- and under-linked pages, internal ranking,
wording diversity, click depth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connect GSC</td><td>Search Console connection and keyword import</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Keys</td><td>Your keys, encrypted at rest, with token usage and spend per key and per model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Settings</td><td>Scoring thresholds, wording length, link density, provider and spend cap</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>15. If you use an API key</h2>

<p>Keys are yours and are stored encrypted. The plugin has no backend of its own: your key goes to the provider
you chose and nowhere else.</p>

<pre class="formula">Tokens per page  =  the fixed instruction (about 420)
                 +  the words of your page you chose to send
                 +  each destination's title and summary
                 +  the reply (about 260)</pre>

<p>The Setup screen shows what one full scan would cost at your current settings, priced for your configured
model, and updates as you change any of the three settings that affect it. It assumes every indexed page is
scanned once; real scans usually cost less, because pages already at their link limit are skipped before the
model is asked anything.</p>

<p>A monthly spend cap pauses AI calls before you overshoot it, and the engine falls back to the free one. The
AI Keys screen reports what was actually billed, which is the authoritative figure &mdash; the estimate is a
guide.</p>

<h2>16. Requirements</h2>

<table>
<tr><th></th><th></th></tr>
<tr><td>WordPress</td><td>6.2 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><td>PHP</td><td>7.4 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><td>An API key</td><td>Optional. Everything except the AI Suggestion engine works without one</td></tr>
<tr><td>Google Search Console</td><td>Optional. Only the GSC keyword engine needs it</td></tr>
</table>

